<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=pasteurisation">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pasteurisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as pasteurization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pasteurisation is the preservation of foods such as milk and fruit juice by mild heat, usually below 100 °C, to kill pathogens and extend shelf life; it is named after the French chemist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-pasteur">Louis Pasteur</a>, who found in 1864, at Arbois, that briefly heating young wine to about 50–60 °C killed its microbes and stopped it turning sour with age. Heating wine for preservation had been known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> since 1117. First used for wine and beer, pasteurisation was applied to milk later, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City made it compulsory for milk in 1910.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pasteurisation.jpg" alt="Pasteurisation" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chicago Department of Health artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Give_The_Bottle-Fed_Baby_a_Chance_For_It's_Life_poster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Heating wine for preservation was known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1117 CE. In 1768 the Italian priest and scientist Lazzaro Spallanzani showed that a product could be made sterile by thermal processing, when meat broth that he boiled for an hour and sealed at once did not spoil. In 1795 the French military offered a prize of 12,000 francs for a new method of preserving food, and the Parisian chef Nicolas Appert won it in January 1810 after some fourteen or fifteen years of experiment, by sealing food in glass jars and boiling them.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-pasteur">Louis Pasteur</a>'s gentler method came in the summer of 1864 at Arbois. He wanted to cure the frequent acidity of the local aged wines, and found that heating young wine to only about 50 to 60 °C for a short time killed the microbes, so that the wine could be aged without loss of quality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Pasteurisation is a mild heat treatment, usually below 100 °C, that destroys or deactivates the microorganisms and enzymes that spoil food or carry disease. Most bacterial spores survive, so it is not sterilisation. The conditions depend on how acidic the food is. In acidic foods such as fruit juice and beer the treatment inactivates enzymes and destroys spoilage microbes, while in milk and liquid eggs it is designed to destroy pathogens as well.</p><p>For milk, high-temperature short-time pasteurisation uses 71.5 °C for 15 seconds and gives a refrigerated shelf life of about two weeks. Ultra-high-temperature treatment at 135 °C for one to two seconds, with special packaging, extends shelf life to three months without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/refrigeration">refrigeration</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Pasteurisation was first used to stop wine and beer souring, and it was many years before milk was treated. As cities grew and supply chains lengthened, raw milk, often days old, came to be recognised as a source of disease. Between 1912 and 1937 some 65,000 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> alone died of tuberculosis contracted from milk. Franz von Soxhlet suggested pasteurising milk in 1886, and in 1910 Ernst Lederle, as Commissioner of Health, introduced mandatory pasteurisation of milk in New York City. Pasteurisation prevents diseases such as tuberculosis, brucellosis and diphtheria, and milk is now considered a safer food.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pasteurization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
